# Square-matrix entry repair

The learner reads the first subscript as row and the second as column, compares both with the matrix order, then copies the intersecting scalar or selects `No such entry`. Tutorial 34f3a63d-a273-50fb-b26a-ae172c7e93e9 explicitly says “The first subscript represents the row and the second subscript represents the column.” Tutorial 775ac64e-8b63-5c51-824d-1d99d17a2a0a defines entries for indices within the dimensions. This requires no arithmetic, even for radicals.

Source bf0cde7f-5b94-5af7-b3da-c7304b62c190 offers all four entries of a 2×2 matrix plus `No such entry` for b32. Canonical fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d asks for the existing b22 in a 2×2 matrix. Hence 2×2 is not evidence of nonexistence. Source 4b55801d-38e7-5449-9bcb-92a485dcdd1c supplies a numeric blank; 3ed21892-cb94-585d-abae-e9ccc908efe4 and 5aee8772-02f1-5e96-b1e0-f67b0c259d43 offer complete radio banks for larger square matrices. All six source cases, including the opaque bank of 0f770f62-3fda-5a7c-ad0a-d63eb715c9d0, remain replayable.

Fixed features have instructional reasons: square shape defines this KP; 2–4 rows retain the easy visual-search workload; one requested scalar retains one lookup; numeric blanks require an existing cell because their contract is numeric; five radio choices preserve the observed response contract. Integers −13…13 and sqrt(2), sqrt(3) preserve source entry forms without arithmetic. The exact matrix name, entries, target location, choice order, and which index is out of range are not mathematically fixed. Missing targets have exactly one index n+1, a conservative extension of the observed missing third row to a missing column and other easy orders.

Answer-bearing roles are the requested coordinate and whether it exists. The default all_four bank can target any of its four cells or either boundary. Larger default banks retain their complete coordinate recipes, but a missing-target question may use an explicitly recorded in-range bank anchor to provide the same four scalar alternatives. The anchor only constructs distractors; the displayed request always determines the key and solution. This is a conservative authored bank extension, not a claim of recovered source intent. It preserves each whole bank, rejects equivalent alternatives together, and keeps the same lookup workload. Existing targets still obey each profile's coordinate prerequisites; blanks cover every existing coordinate. Source-specific q-* and raw_q-* routes preserve their original banks and presentation.

Choose the existence case before value rejection, independent of size/layout. A radio bank chosen for a finite default easy batch is instantiated as a pair: one existing target and one missing target, sharing size, matrix, name, choices, and layout. Shuffle the full output; an odd remaining slot is a numeric blank. This is a decision-case coverage obligation for a small pool, not a numerical answer balance quota. Single-item requests cannot contain a counterexample pair and retain stochastic selection. Explicit source replay remains available. Check both raw output and shared delivery survivors: every represented radio size/layout group must contain both cases when the CLI can produce pairs. This prevents the retained failure without selecting a special seed. Within-template probes also inspect answer position and labels; controlled target and irrelevant-cell changes check dependent text and grading.

Requested easy is source-supported. Existing moderate/hard 5×5/6×6 routes are retained unchanged as prior authored visual-search extensions, not newly certified source difficulty bands. No new band or method is introduced by this repair. Source defects remain recorded: raw q-125593 historically names E in the solution (learner output corrects it to R); q-13506 omits difficulty, key, and solution (normal completion infers the entry and labels its authored easy assignment); q-13490 contains two opaque scalars, replayed without inventing distractor intent. Supplied source has no diagrams and no separate prerequisite records. TeX matrices are not image assets.
